add('Meet the Python GitHub repository',None,[('strong a','Udemy-Claude-Agent-SDK-python','Welcome to the actual Python learning repository on GitHub. Check that the owner is luiscoco and the name ends with Agent SDK python. This is a different application from the TypeScript student platform.'),('a',SHA[:7],'The recording is based on the revision shown here. To obtain the project, use the green Code menu to copy its HTTPS address, clone it with Git, and enter the repository directory in your terminal. Future commits may change the instructions.')])
add('What the Python edition teaches','Claude Agent SDK Learning Platform Python edition',[('article h1','Claude Agent SDK Learning Platform Python edition','Start with the README. This edition teaches the official Python Claude Agent SDK through seventy-five numbered exercises and a browser lab authored in Python.'),('article p','The stack is','Its stack uses FastAPI and Uvicorn for the service, NiceGUI for the browser interface, Pydantic for validation, and asyncio for asynchronous work. The project also uses the official MCP Python SDK and a Python testing and tooling environment.')])
add('Install and start the platform','Install and start',[('article p','Use Python 3.12 or newer','Install Python three point twelve or newer and uv. The README records verification with Python three point fourteen. Follow the committed lockfile to reproduce dependencies instead of installing arbitrary versions.'),('article pre','uv sync --locked','From the repository directory, run uv sync locked, then uv run agent lab serve. These are setup instructions shown from the actual documentation; this browser recording is not a terminal installation session.'),('article p','Open http://127.0.0.1:8080','Open the local browser interface on port eighty eighty. Each lesson offers teaching notes, executable Python source, and offline or live operation. Response and results presents readable output, while Diagnostics keeps raw events available for lessons that inspect them. FastAPI documentation is available at the docs path.')])
add('Run your first lesson independently','Run lessons independently',[('article pre','uv run agent-lab list','Use agent lab list to see the curriculum, then run lesson one from the command line. You can also invoke a numbered lesson module directly, as the lesson-five example shows. The browser and these entry points share the same implementation.'),('article p','Offline exercises execute','Offline exercises run real local host code without calling Claude. Recorded messages, usage values, and scripted workers are labeled fixtures. Exercise evidence remains under the run-specific lab data working directory; the worktree example requires Git.')])
LESSON=URL+'/blob/main/Tab1-query().md'
add('Follow the Lesson 1 tutorial','Lesson 1: query()',[('article p','Consume an asynchronous','The first lesson teaches how to consume an asynchronous message iterator and distinguish assistant text from the final result. Begin by predicting what evidence you expect to see.'),('article pre','uv run agent-lab lesson 1','The tutorial provides both a CLI command and a standalone module command. Both run offline host code. To use the interface instead, start the server and open the lesson-one link.'),('article p','The exercise is in','The document links directly to the exercise, host contracts, and permission policy. Follow these links when you want to understand or change the behavior instead of treating the screen as a black box.')],LESSON)
add('Inspect Python message handling','What to inspect',[('article pre','async def message_exercise','This is real Python source displayed in the GitHub tutorial. A recorded async generator produces an assistant message and a result message. The loop collects text blocks separately from result metadata. Notice the explicit fixture label: no SDK process is started by this recorded example.')],LESSON)
add('Configure optional live access','Enable live SDK calls',[('article p','Windows:','For optional live use, copy the example environment file to your local dot env file. Configure your own Anthropic key, enable live operation, and select the model. The key on screen is a documentation placeholder, not a usable credential.'),('article pre','LAB_ENABLE_LIVE=true','The host requires the live-enable setting before accepting paid SDK work. Keep actual credentials private and restart the server after configuration changes.'),('article p','Restart the server. Live mode','Live mode uses the official Agent SDK and its Claude Code runtime, rather than the general Messages API client. Each run has its own working directory and CLI configuration. The lab is configured not to reuse personal login settings or repository settings.')])
add('Check the runtime before live work','Enable live SDK calls',[('article pre','uv run agent-lab doctor','Run agent lab doctor before authorizing paid calls. It checks the SDK and executable installation without making a model request. Executable discovery is a prerequisite, not evidence of a successful paid response.'),('article p','Some platform/source distributions','Some SDK distributions omit the bundled executable. The README describes this case for Windows. Install native Claude Code using the official instructions, verify its version, and set LAB CLI PATH if automatic lookup cannot find it.'),('article pre','Add 19 and 23','The documented live example asks lesson five to add nineteen and twenty-three with the calculator tool. Read that lesson and its controls before trying it. The SDK budget is a threshold, and the local teaching host is not an authenticated shared service.')])
add('Understand the shared application architecture','Architecture',[('article tr','agent_lab/curriculum.py','The curriculum module defines stable lesson identities and objectives. Numbered modules provide standalone entry points, exercises contains local host demonstrations, and services holds shared business contracts and data behavior.'),('article tr','agent_lab/sdk.py','SDK integration lives in sdk dot py. Runtime owns tasks, deadlines, and cancellation; API provides JSON, server-sent events, and WebSockets; the NiceGUI interface provides lesson navigation and controls.'),('article p','The UI, CLI and API call one service','The UI, command line, and API call the same service. SDK connections are opened, consumed, and closed within one owning task. Cancellation waits for cleanup, and tool input does not itself grant access to arbitrary files or another customer.')])
add('Explore the actual Python source folder','api.py',[('a','api.py','Here is the real agent lab package on GitHub. The module names let you move from transport to runtime, SDK integration, policy, and interface code. Use Go to file or the folder tree to locate the module behind a lesson before making a change.')],URL+'/tree/main/agent_lab')
add('Connect lessons to API transports','API and lifecycle',[('article tr','Collected JSON events','A lesson exposes metadata, a collected JSON run, a server-sent event stream, and WebSocket events with cancellation. These are different transports over the same lesson service.'),('article pre','"mode": "offline"','Pydantic validates the request before execution. This example selects offline mode and supplies turn and budget settings. A live request needs both the live mode selection and the backend live-enable configuration.'),('article p','All transports use','RunService bounds concurrency and owns the producer task. Each run emits a terminal done event, and cancellation awaits cleanup. When reading results, distinguish completion from cancellation, failure, and incomplete output.')],LESSON)
add('Explore optional MCP and browser adapters','Optional integrations',[('article pre','agent_lab.integrations.notes_mcp','The optional integrations include a notes MCP server, an agent MCP server, and a remote MCP inspector. Start them explicitly. The agent server needs live access enabled, and the remote inspector requires a separately running trusted server.'),('article pre','--extra browser','For the browser adapter, install the browser extra and Playwright Chromium, then start the browser MCP module. Its requests are restricted to the configured browser origin. These adapters have their own setup requirements.')])
add('Choose browser or desktop mode','For a native window',[('article p','It allows requests only','The browser adapter is constrained by LAB BROWSER ORIGIN. Notification adapters need supplied credentials and are not invoked automatically at application startup. Offline exercises do not send those notifications.'),('article p','For a native window','Browser mode is the standard course setup. If you want a native window, install the desktop extra and use serve native. You may need additional platform-specific pywebview dependencies. This is an optional execution surface, not a separate set of lessons.')])
add('Verify your changes and rebuild lesson docs','Verify and rebuild',[('article pre','uv run ruff check','The repository lists four checks: Ruff for linting, mypy for typing, pytest for regression tests, and the curriculum documentation builder. Run the checks appropriate to your changes, and use the lockfile to keep the environment consistent.'),('article p','The documentation build uses','The builder uses the curriculum contract, indexed document names, and exercise source. It does not need the recovery archive. Tests require no API credentials, and paid model calls remain separate opt-in work.')])
add('Read the migration scope honestly','Migration coverage and recovery',[('article p','This edition reimplements','The Python edition reimplements seventy-five learning objectives with a shared app and streamlined exercises. It is not yet feature-for-feature equivalent to every original control, live experiment, and production workflow.'),('article p','The autonomous engineering capstone','The engineering capstone uses scripted workers offline and SDK agents in live mode. Full worktree merge and recovery, publication workflows, and several specialized live experiments still require implementation or verification. Read the lesson adaptations instead of assuming parity.'),('article p','Lesson 41 teaches','Python-specific topics include the public interactive client and desktop embedding. The legacy runtime and Node dependencies are removed from the active project; the preserved archive is recovery material, not the running application.')])
add('Your next steps','Practice',[('article ol','Identify trusted host inputs','Start with lesson one offline. Identify trusted host inputs and untrusted model or source data. Change one bounded input, predict validation, and add a failure case. Then compare offline evidence with an explicitly authorized live run when you are ready.'),('article p','uv run pytest','Keep the tutorial, implementation, and evidence together. Use the tests to check your changes, and use the documentation links to investigate SDK behavior. A model describing an action is not proof that the action happened.')],LESSON)
